e68-7fcbd3e64787" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ddda73a-66ad-5c78-97bd-db73c90fdc26" :question/math-academy-id "q-100187" :question/problem "Simplify $(-17)^{0}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n(-17)^{0}=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "69f97c9d-1171-5397-90c7-026c8b339a1f"}] :activity/title "The Zeroth Power" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541421}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139294} :activity/id #uuid "88d9417f-4dcb-5b07-99a4-4fc3631a5617" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438 :topic/id #uuid "9f62517d-bdce-5662-9e1b-19d15df5109b" :topic/math-academy-id 2234} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139715 :tutorial/content "We always introduce **errors** in our measurements whenever we round or approximate numbers. In this lesson, we will explore how to quantify and describe measurement errors.\n\nSuppose that a particular rope measures precisely $48.9\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ Now, imagine we take a meterstick and record a measurement of $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ *to the nearest $10$ centimeters*. Let's mark this approximation on our meterstick.\n\n![](images/4e/4e5d5ca05f27aa612b3fdcc9430b495870ddf540630857b6227897d6b20c9e89.png)\n\nThe error of a measurement equals the difference between the rounded estimate and the \"true\" value. So, in this example, the error of our approximation equals\n\n$$\n50\\,\\textrm{cm} - 48.9\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1.1\\textrm{cm}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe often don't know the \"true\" measurement in practical problems. So, the next best thing is to determine a **lower bound** and an **upper bound** for our measurement:\n\n- The *lower* bound of a measurement is its *smallest* possible value.\n\n- The *upper* bound of a measurement is its *largest* possible value.\n\nLet's figure out the lower and upper bounds of our $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ approximation:\n\n- The *lower bound* is $45 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is the *smallest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/da/da9cd6208cef81c6fe34fbba9b4271fa3ce1f30df69eb3c631ce6fea4f12e3a3.png)\n\n- The *upper bound* is $55 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is (almost) the *largest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](images/23/23a195d88326b587e1864ef04e7b05f74d675f1ddc987baa2cf586af599eb4a9.png)\n\nLet's use the letter $l$ to denote the \"true\" length of the rope. Then, we can combine our lower and upper bound measurements into a single statement, as follows:\n\n$$\n45\\,\\textrm{cm} \\leq l \\lt 55\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used a *strict* \"<\" inequality for the upper bound. We cannot include $55\\textrm{cm}$ in our set of values because this number rounds to $60\\,\\textrm{cm},$ not $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\nWe can visualize the lower and upper bounds on our diagram as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fdacb0787e8316a78da1ad65aa177e9a6f29215fa0d53aef88b0eb33326218d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d44fcde5-ad8a-596b-a6ac-955ff40b6bd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8751 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cc0c262d-dd9e-52a1-a83b-9f1c0fe96262" :step/math-academy-id 165169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139716 :tutorial/content "So, we've seen how to calculate lower and upper bounds. Before we consider another example, note the following:\n\n- The lower and upper bound depend on our approximation's **degree of accuracy**.\n\n- In the last example, the degree of accuracy was $10\\,\\textrm{cm}$ because the rope was measured to the nearest $10$ centimeters.\n\n- If we change the degree of accuracy, the lower and upper bounds will also change. So, for example, if we measured the rope to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{cm},$ the lower and upper bounds would be different.\n\n- The **greatest possible error** is the largest amount the \"true\" value can be from the approximation.\n\n- This greatest possible error is always one-half of the degree of accuracy. So, in the last example, the greatest possible error in our measurement was\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{cm}}{2} = {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the greatest possible error to compute the lower and upper bounds quickly:\n\n- To compute the lower bound of an approximation, we *subtract* the greatest possible error from the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{lower bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} - {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 45\\,\\textrm{cm.}\n$$\n\n- To compute the upper bound of an approximation, we *add* the greatest possible error to the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{upper bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} + {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 55\\,\\textrm{cm.}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "f1808067-d542-577c-a2d3-085e0fb1df59" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8752 :tutorial/title "The Greatest Possible Error"} :step/id #uuid "66ba1355-ab27-5d81-8157-a359b9801285" :step/math-academy-id 165170 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138387 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138164 :question/id #uuid "b18fef84-bbbb-58c6-a215-4146461c28f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-14084"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61a5c363-0371-5820-a4e4-b21335406e50" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050051}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476950}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "bf5aa060-ae70-531a-8e1c-d165c1d25adb" :step/math-academy-id 165171 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138388 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138165 :question/id #uuid "9e0f0485-0635-558e-8493-eef61ee623f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14085"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e99cabd4-b713-50e1-83be-f8571b575803" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050934}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536858}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Decimal Place"} :step/id #uuid "7d898aa1-1cb4-5977-87b3-02ae7082146a" :step/math-academy-id 165174 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138389 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138167 :question/id #uuid "44ff13ac-1980-554e-aea8-4aaa51d202fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14087"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29217571-4597-5bbe-aecd-0d9a1d1aa1c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050792}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539011}] :knowledge-point/title "Bounding Areas and Volumes"} :step/id #uuid "761a41b0-39e8-54bb-9403-886bbe9ed521" :step/math-academy-id 165177}] :activity/title "Degrees of Accuracy" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138593}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539064} :activity/id #uuid "b2fa5316-e98c-5047-902c-0d6c438a9d06" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048438 :topic/id #uuid "9f62517d-bdce-5662-9e1b-19d15df5109b" :topic/math-academy-id 2234} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "255bade8-0a89-5de2-8a24-9fd1e2100b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.3\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538308} {:answer/id #uuid "31e67d7e-4e60-5f06-8489-9ee7a57c5092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.7\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538335} {:answer/id #uuid "995c6b46-9527-5d81-9c6e-1f7397ab9bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14.2\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538593} {:answer/id #uuid "a64f0aa8-5dbd-5dd3-ae18-7c76124f8c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.0\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538631} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fd67f7-1c22-5e81-be9b-2698dfa44075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.5\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538631} :answer-field/id #uuid "278391dd-df9b-5ab8-b856-03ca04c5de44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cc1f868e-acd2-53b5-a61f-6b065116ee9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-46386" :question/problem "The dimensions of a cubic water tank are $2.4\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{m},$  where each dimension is measured to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ Rounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter, what is the smallest possible volume of the tank?" :question/worked-solution "Each dimension is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible volume of the tank. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 2.4\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m}=2.35\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible volume of the tank is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2.35\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.35\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.35\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =12.977\\,875\\,{\\text{m}}^{3} \\\\  & ≈13.0\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter."} :step/id #uuid "59367d0f-feb4-57b0-bee3-c4cdea97ec6b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2acee517-33dc-5f55-a0b3-f02e6c6263fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538323} :answer-field/id #uuid "62968f0c-410e-58a3-b547-3822b3d5961c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a0cc887e-fde1-561f-a2a2-0e9220220d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-210524" :question/problem "The dimensions of a rectangular floor are $12\\,\\text{m}\\times 3\\,\\text{m}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $1\\,\\text{m}.$ Rounded to the nearest square meter, what is the smallest possible area of the floor?\n\nTo the nearest square meter, the smallest possible area is\n{{field-1}}${\\text{m}}^{2}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Each dimension is given to the nearest $1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.5\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible area of the container. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 12\\,\\text{m}-0.5\\,\\text{m}=11.5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 3\\,\\text{m}-0.5\\,\\text{m}=2.5\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible area of the container is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =11.5\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =28.75\\,{\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈29\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest square meter."} :step/id #uuid "67e2fe74-1f9c-5f39-b367-3beb48c1832e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16fac51a-9457-5261-b22b-f89d79af0f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.09\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.11\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538282} {:answer/id #uuid "2e710c8a-03cf-5964-8f07-e6abecb91318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.0\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.2\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538329} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4403cc-a3af-50f8-91d2-7e006f9ca2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.095\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.105\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538391} {:answer/id #uuid "61ad7ba7-c6e6-5a77-88a2-8f331b4e6cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.05\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.15\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538448} {:answer/id #uuid "de59b963-7146-502e-8106-8eeb734cdb88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.1\\,\\text{m}\\le h<3.1\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538448} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b01a9c5-17a4-5cf7-9eed-2c1fcee823f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "66b93aa5-a873-551c-b039-7debf6d7af79" :question/math-academy-id "q-154080" :question/problem "The shoulder height of a moose $h$ measures $2.1\\,\\text{m}$ to the nearest tenth of a meter. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." :question/worked-solution "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{lower bound} & =2.1\\,\\text{m}-0.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =2.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\ \\text{upper bound} & =2.1\\,\\text{m}+0.05\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =2.15\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $h$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2.05\\,\\text{m}\\le h<2.15\\,\\text{m}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "afdc7c1a-9e06-55c0-8af8-0603e546f8f9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539066}}] :activity/title "Degrees of Accuracy" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538226}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153298} :activity/id #uuid "b42cbc99-dc3a-5ea5-af15-2e43d6dacdce" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048502 :topic/id #uuid "a4f450cf-8fc5-50db-946a-e2e69ec67915" :topic/math-academy-id 2560} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153325 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we need to find a number that is a certain percentage *greater than* a given amount. This corresponds to applying a so-called **percentage increase.**\n\nTo apply a percentage increase, we add the increase to $100\\%,$ convert the result to a decimal multiplier, and then find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following problem.\n\nWhat number is $25\\%$ *greater than* $160?$\n\nThe key words here are \"$\\boldsymbol{25\\%}$ **greater than.**\" This means we need to apply a percentage increase, increasing the original number by $25\\%.$\n\nTo find a number $25\\%$ greater than $160,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 25\\% = 125\\%.\n$$\n\nThis means that our new number is $125\\%$ of $160.$ \n\nSo, first, we convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n125\\% = \\dfrac{125}{100} = 1.25\n$$\n\nTo find a number that is $25\\%$ greater than $160,$ we multiply $160$ by $1.25{:}$\n\n$$\n1.25 \\cdot 160 = 200\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $25\\%$ *greater than* $160$ is $200.$\n\nThis method applies to *any* percentage increase, including those greater than $100\\%{;}$ add the increase to $100\\%$ before converting to a decimal multiplier." :tutorial/id #uuid "83dbd64f-3ab9-5239-b54b-ae6d91816248" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14336 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "547ba33b-b6b3-5bae-b2dc-eacefe1e81a4" :step/math-academy-id 615692 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152295 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151899 :question/id #uuid "2cfd1ed7-8249-5970-9ddc-1bb69929951c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21450"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "125ed0b3-14ad-5af8-8370-ca6f83d93c35" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050716}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455469}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Percentage Increase"} :step/id #uuid "a946c7fa-5aee-54d2-b223-0a3b560b3401" :step/math-academy-id 615693 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153300}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152296 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151900 :question/id #uuid "95c3dedd-accd-57dc-91ee-4ee8fdb2f3a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21451"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f90ea994-79f2-5409-bf96-306803b4ef01" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049467}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542120}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Percentage Increase: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "fd57418f-a99a-5c17-9192-9191eca0084b" :step/math-academy-id 615696 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153340 :tutorial/content "It's possible to increase a number by $100\\%$ or more. \n\nFor example, what number is $150\\%$ greater than $90?$\n\nTo find a number $150\\%$ *greater than* $90,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 150\\% = 250\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250\\% = \\dfrac{250}{100} = 2.5\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $150\\%$ greater than $90,$ we multiply $90$ by $2.5:$\n\n$$\n2.5 \\cdot 90 = 225\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $150\\%$ greater than $90$ is $225.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a0514684-2b6b-52ce-9ccb-6d28ed8d4850" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15090 :tutorial/title "Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "a3bd6e9e-9d93-51a6-9a20-5e9292cf93f4" :step/math-academy-id 615700 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152297 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151901 :question/id #uuid "4af9bc31-4980-5c3c-9025-45e85ac09d8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21517"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88d0f4ad-a3f9-5275-b5d5-a9973bf63ebf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050618}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536824}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "d9b4f520-6201-594a-907d-e7a899c8d57c" :step/math-academy-id 615701 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153303}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152298 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151913 :question/id #uuid "92d9c3ca-6e59-5c73-b640-114dfb7d13cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-22093"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbb0103d-d686-575a-8ef8-81640227f4dc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455511}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Word Problems Involving Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "10eca415-b601-5bc8-80c2-c2db5a9c451e" :step/math-academy-id 615704}] :activity/title "Applying Percentage Increases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152315}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486523} :activity/id #uuid "332f39d2-fe5b-5fe5-bfa7-8da0dfbe5393" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048502 :topic/id #uuid "a4f450cf-8fc5-50db-946a-e2e69ec67915" :topic/math-academy-id 2560} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a39d413-aa71-523a-b6b3-dae4d712baf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485945} :answer-field/id #uuid "290d7c72-0a37-54c1-928d-b289c4e12639" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cf1e4b17-b634-5d17-aa2d-098e82d8022f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327654" :question/problem "A factory produced $900$ boxes of cereal last year. This year, production is $300%$ greater than last year. How many boxes of cereal did the factory produce this year?\n\nThe factory produced\n{{field-1}}\nboxes of cereal this year." :question/worked-solution "To find this year's cereal production, we apply a $300%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $300%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+300%=400%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n400%=\\frac{400}{100}=4\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $4:$\n\n$$\n900\\times 4=3,600\n$$\n\nSo, the factory produced $3600$ boxes of cereal this year."} :step/id #uuid "00e71b2a-765d-5d7f-a25b-d91d73442118"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63a00a9b-f175-5563-a7e5-e9656e27a637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485935} {:answer/id #uuid "758ee6d2-143b-5d0c-85c7-1b00d67826f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485969} {:answer/id #uuid "974abbd8-6ac1-5b5c-809b-ef8a1790dbd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486038} {:answer/id #uuid "aca33ef9-b028-5c98-a62b-f95962819c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486076} {:answer/id #uuid "be2c74de-5ea2-597a-8fc7-7510c00cdf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486103}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486038} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3f1f35-966b-596c-862d-3ab162b9470c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa7ba5f1-091b-5461-b064-b9a572c0acc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-318357" :question/problem "What number is $155%$ greater than $40?$" :question/worked-solution "To find a number $155%$ *greater than* $40,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+155%=255%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n255%=\\frac{255}{100}=2.55\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $155%$ greater than $40,$ we multiply $40$ by $2.55:$\n\n$$\n2.55\\cdot 40=102\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $155%$ greater than $40$ is $102.$"} :step/id #uuid "aa852896-f641-5bf3-81c3-1ddb33a1c5d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e18203-4dbf-5257-84e1-5d825b64f9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,000,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485764} {:answer/id #uuid "01c47803-fa37-5ad4-a822-f030d4115e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,050,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485767} {:answer/id #uuid "22fa57f8-4bd0-583b-b691-d351b66f3152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,075,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485824} {:answer/id #uuid "26239f53-9cc0-56a7-acb2-66f332c11f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,100,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485828} {:answer/id #uuid "623d64e1-d70f-5584-bac1-b87f99bf2302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,025,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485934}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485824} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4f452c-1a22-5679-9e96-6dcc369d76d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ceab7d75-8909-5909-a769-ff36bb9c7667" :question/math-academy-id "q-326524" :question/problem "A country welcomed $3,\\,500,\\,000$ tourists in $2018.$  Travel authorities predict that this number will increase by $45%$ by the year $2028.$ Which of the following is closest to the projected number of tourists in $2028?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the projected number of tourists in $2028,$ we apply a $45%$ increase to the $2018$ figure.\n\nIncreasing by $45%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+45%=145%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n145%=\\frac{145}{100}=1.45\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the new value by multiplying the original amount by $1.45:$\n\n$$\n3,\\,500,\\,000\\times 1.45=5,\\,075,\\,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projected number of tourists in $2028$ is $5,\\,075,\\,000.$"} :step/id #uuid "ad0e7bc5-f3f5-5418-b7be-7515dd52c1f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486464}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2bf66f-071b-599c-870d-beb08d151c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$14.00$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485789} {:answer/id #uuid "81d65c1e-4fea-5d3d-b067-2a903bca796a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$12.75$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485993} {:answer/id #uuid "9a524f5e-5406-5bdc-81e7-caf6bc6d50a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$12.95$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486041} {:answer/id #uuid "f89dfbd4-8787-51f2-b071-d16433f03c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$13.50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486213} {:answer/id #uuid "fccdd420-a0f2-5e8c-abe0-1561c37428e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$13.14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486226} :answer-field/id #uuid "901b558e-ee47-587e-b837-3417a076e126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6f6e2bde-069f-5dbd-8445-437abb3c38f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-326517" :question/problem "A sandwich costs $$12$ before tax. If a $9.5%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total price after tax?" :question/worked-solution "To find the total price after tax, we apply a $9.5%$ increase to the original cost.\n\nIncreasing by $9.5%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+9.5%=109.5%=1.095\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.095:$\n\n$$\n12\\times 1.095=13.14.\n$$\n\nSo, the total price after tax is $$13.14.$"} :step/id #uuid "af48d2fc-c0ee-5116-9e15-35ee93f0388c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a7cc659-73e4-532b-99a7-3c634766ec34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485946} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9ddf9b-1b91-530e-8514-164da8ccae4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485981} {:answer/id #uuid "b58bfefd-ae8c-59b0-b73a-de23da9e8c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486088} {:answer/id #uuid "c94777e7-dc40-5649-ab0e-9b864344dabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486127} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ed7cc-6a7f-517f-944e-d4397979f27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485946} :answer-field/id #uuid "a94424b9-97e6-5016-9825-443b370a2c58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "62ed2686-5fd2-570f-9e2c-651f1d63d801" :question/math-academy-id "q-318127" :question/problem "What number is $125%$ greater than $64?$" :question/worked-solution "To find a number $125%$ *greater than* $64,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+125%=225%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n225%=\\frac{225}{100}=2.25\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $125%$ greater than $64,$ we multiply $64$ by $2.25:$\n\n$$\n2.25\\cdot 64=144\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $125%$ greater than $64$ is $144.$"} :step/id #uuid "ff1f3d10-46ad-5cfc-b250-e2375bf213df" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486503}}] :activity/title "Applying Percentage Increases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485754}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134917} :activity/id #uuid "b257a2d9-a21e-5e7b-8297-daf3ed7e1fae" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048868 :topic/id #uuid "c7dda967-adbc-5d28-94d6-a574e5483086" :topic/math-academy-id 2184} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135015 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to evaluate the expression $\\sqrt x + 3$ for the value $x=9.$ This type of expression is called a **radical expression** because it contains the radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$ Remember that $\\sqrt x$ means \"the positive square root of the number $x.$\" \n\nTo evaluate this expression at the given value, we replace $x$ with $9$ in the given expression and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x}+3 & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9}+3 & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{3}^{2}}+3 & = \\\\ 3+3 & = \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** You may have heard somewhere that $\\sqrt{9} = \\pm 3,$ because $(-3)^2=9$ and $(3)^2 = 9.$ However, when we write down $\\sqrt{9},$ we *always* mean \"the positive square root of $9$\".\n\nIf we want the negative square root of $9$, we write $-\\sqrt{9} = -3.$ Sometimes, we want both, in which case we would write $\\pm\\sqrt{9}=\\pm 3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4457c20d-88c9-5028-8390-390579fa7229" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1610 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9851ef2d-c687-52a6-8ea1-ff43bff4d334" :step/math-academy-id 4338 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134918}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133524 :question/id #uuid "6488c7bd-4179-5b78-bdfc-92973f9a141f" :question/math-academy-id "e-1375"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "779a527b-c35d-5c94-b783-2e6eb117f00a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483381}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Radical Expression that Simplifies to a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "87051a9a-ddb4-5816-97f9-79ce959ac930" :step/math-academy-id 4339 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133919 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133525 :question/id #uuid "6c9e9059-c72a-5cd4-94ee-33cabb839cab" :question/math-academy-id "e-1377"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63a2c63d-2329-5679-b426-eea89828b39a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496302}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Radical Expression that Does Not Simplify to